From af619fe80de30633634c3b51a299fd351644dd66 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Antumbra build <antumbra@localhost>
Date: Mon, 5 Oct 2026 11:21:22 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] media: qcom: hotdog-popup: retract before sleep and shutdown,
 bound the course in time

The pop-up camera motor is a genpd provider whose power_on and power_off
callbacks run the opening and closing courses. Several paths could leave
the camera raised or drive it past its limits.

System sleep. genpd powers a domain that was off back on in resume_noirq
(genpd_finish_resume -> genpd_sync_power_on) and ignores the result, and
it powers an active domain off in suspend_noirq. Both run while the GENI
I2C bus is unusable: the open attempt fails on the Hall read (-EACCES in
the port's suspend log), and a camera raised at suspend cannot be closed
and stays up. A PM notifier now retracts a camera that is not closed at
PM_SUSPEND_PREPARE (and the hibernation equivalents), while tasks and
I2C still run, and sets a sleeping flag. While it is set, the genpd
callbacks only record the state genpd asks for and never move. After
resume, a reconcile work item raises the camera again when the domain is
on and a consumer was active when genpd powered it back on, so a stream
held across sleep runs with the camera up again. power_off on a camera
the Halls show as seated stays a no-op success.

Whether a consumer holds the camera is tracked apart from genpd's view
of the domain: a successful power_on sets it, every power_off clears it
(genpd powers the domain off only once every consumer is suspended, so a
close that fails leaves the domain on with no consumer), and a gated
power_on reads it from the consumers' runtime state. The reconcile, and
the reopen request recorded at PM_SUSPEND_PREPARE, follow the consumer,
not the domain: after a failed close followed by a suspend aborted
before the noirq phases (a wakeup during freezing, a later PM notifier
refusing), the camera is no longer raised with no stream. A retract at
PM_SUSPEND_PREPARE that fails, or whose Hall read fails, is retried by
the reconcile after resume when no consumer wants the camera up; the
gated noirq callbacks would otherwise record the domain off and leave
the camera raised. An ungated power_on or power_off, or the final
retract, supersedes the retry. A close course that takes no step, the
Halls already reading the camera seated, no longer gets the settle push:
at a stop that reads |down| 340-349 that push could not raise the
reading, and power_off failed with -EPROTO and kept the domain on.

Shutdown. A reboot notifier, which runs before device_shutdown() with
every bus up, and a .shutdown fallback retract a camera that is not
closed, as the stock driver does, then refuse any further motion.
Driver removal does the same.

Probe. Once both Hall channels resolve, a camera found out of its closed
position with plausible readings is retracted, so recovery no longer
waits for the IMX471, CCI and CAMSS drivers to probe and idle.

Course budget. The course limit summed usleep intervals while LPG STEP
kept pulsing through Hall reads that block for up to 25 ms each (longer
on a hung bus), so a course that never reached its endpoint could run
well past 44160 microsteps. Microsteps are now counted from wall-clock
time and the periods the PWM hardware reports: the slow ramp, then the
fast course. A course ends at 44160 microsteps or at a wall-clock cap of
the nominal course (about 668 ms) plus 50 ms, whichever comes first. An
hrtimer watchdog enforces the same deadline from a work item, by driving
nSLEEP low and disabling STEP, even while the stepping thread is blocked
in a Hall read; that thread then restores the complete safe pin state.
The fixed-length slow runs get the same watchdog. A STEP period faster
than stock (104166 ns ramp, 12000 ns course) is refused; the port's
105000 and 13125 ns are already slower. A course that ends on the cap
reports -ETIMEDOUT instead of -ERANGE, and the elapsed time is logged
and shown in status.

Binding. The DTS and the driver name the pinctrl states "gpio" and
"pwm"; the binding required "default" and "pwm", which dtbs_check flags
on the hotdog DTB.

Attack surface. status reads the Hall sensors, which tells whether the
front camera is in use, so it is now readable by root only. The
write-once bring-up knobs (pulse_up, restore_closed, open, finish_open,
close) move the motor outside the camera lifecycle; they now exist only
with the debug_knobs=1 module parameter and refuse to run once sleep or
shutdown has begun.

Unchanged: 1/32 microstepping (M1 high, M0 high-Z), at least 1 ms from
nSLEEP to the first STEP, the 960-microstep slow ramp, the Hall endpoint
stop, the |Hall| > 450 and no-progress aborts, the single retry of a
missed close ramp, and STEP off with nSLEEP, DIR and M1 low after every
move.

Carried by the Antumbra project; not upstream. Needs validation on the
phone (Antumbra docs/hardware-validation.md).
---
